                <pb facs="0159" n="152" sameAs="frus:frus1894Nicaragua/pg152"/>

                <head>No. 88.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Baker to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-08-08"><hi rendition="#italic">August 8, 1894</hi></date>.
                        (Received September 4.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On the evening of August 2 Mr. Gustavo
                    Guzman came to this legation bearing, as he informed me, a verbal message from
                    the President, to the following effect: <list>
                        <item>First. That the Government had sent a large number of troops to <pb
                                facs="0160" n="153" xml:id="pg153"/> San Juan del Norte, where they
                            had just arrived, on their way to Blue-fields.</item>
                        <item>Second. That this Government had chartered the steamboat <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Yulu</hi>, a boat owned by the Emory Company of
                            Boston, flying the United States flag, to transport these troops from
                            San Juan del Norte to Bluefields.</item>
                        <item>Third. That now the captain and crew of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Yulu</hi>, all Americans, refuse to carry the soldiers, for the
                            reason that Commander O’Neil, of the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Marblehead</hi>, had issued a proclamation forbidding vessels under
                            the flag of the United States from “carrying bodies of armed men or
                            military supplies” for either “party” to the controversy in the Mosquito
                            territory.</item>
                        <item>Fourth. The President, therefore, requested that I, as United States
                            minister, issue an order to the captain and crew of the steamer <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Yulu</hi>, assuring them that they run no risk
                            in disobeying the warning of Commander O’Neil.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I could not believe it to be my duty to comply with this request; but, at the
                    suggestion of Mr. Guzman, I gave him the accompanying telegram, marked Inclosure
                    No. 1, which he had liberty to send if he so desired. Inclosure No. 2 is a copy
                    of the proclamation of Commander O’Neil referred to.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lewis Baker.</hi></signed></closer>



